      "description": "During the end step, KimiK2 Vorthos chose to evoke Solitude (exiling a white card from hand) but has no targets on the opponent's battlefield to exile — GPT5n Spike has no creatures in play. Meanwhile, they could have used Psychic Frog's discard ability to pump it (discarding Atraxa to grow Frog and set up Goryo's Vengeance on Atraxa next turn), or cracked Flooded Strand to fix mana. Evoking Solitude with no meaningful target wastes a premium removal spell. More importantly, the reasoning text suggests confusion — they say 'pass and move to opponent's turn' but actually chose Solitude, suggesting a misclick or misunderstanding of what they were doing.",
      "action_taken": "Evoked Solitude during the end step with no opponent creatures on the battlefield to exile.",
      "better_line": "Use Psychic Frog's activated ability to discard Atraxa, Grand Unifier (pumping Frog to 2/3 and putting Atraxa in graveyard for Goryo's Vengeance), or simply crack Flooded Strand for mana fixing, saving all three Solitudes for when the opponent actually has threats.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 90,
      "player": "KimiK2 Vorthos",
      "decision_index": 62,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Discarding Goryo's Vengeance to pump Psychic Frog is wasteful. Goryo's Vengeance is a powerful instant that can reanimate Atraxa, Grand Unifier (a legendary creature in the graveyard) at instant speed, providing a massive 7/7 with flying, vigilance, deathtouch, lifelink and drawing multiple cards from its ETB trigger. The player has two copies and is discarding one for a mere +1/+1 counter on a Frog that is already tapped and done attacking this turn. While having a second copy mitigates this somewhat, throwing away such a high-impact card for incremental damage is a poor trade, especially when there are lower-value cards in hand (like Plains) that could be discarded instead if pumping was desired.",
      "action_taken": "Activated Psychic Frog by discarding Goryo's Vengeance to put a +1/+1 counter on it.",
      "better_line": "If wanting to pump Psychic Frog, discard Plains instead (a basic land with low utility given existing mana base). Preserve both copies of Goryo's Vengeance to threaten instant-speed Atraxa reanimation, which represents a far more impactful play than +1/+1 on the Frog.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 101,
      "player": "KimiK2 Vorthos",
      "decision_index": 70,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Casting Goryo's Vengeance during their own combat phase means Atraxa will be exiled at the beginning of their own end step this turn, getting only the ETB trigger. If cast during the opponent's end step instead, Atraxa would survive through their next turn, allowing a 7/7 flying lifelink deathtouch attack (gaining 7 life and dealing 7 damage) before being exiled at the beginning of that turn's end step.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Goryo's Vengeance targeting Atraxa during their own combat phase, getting only the ETB card selection.",
      "better_line": "Hold Goryo's Vengeance and cast it during the opponent's end step to get both the ETB trigger AND a full attack step with a 7/7 flying lifelink deathtouch creature, representing a 14-point life swing plus the card advantage.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 112,
      "player": "KimiK2 Vorthos",
      "decision_index": 76,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Failed to crack Flooded Strand before Atraxa's ETB trigger resolves. Thinning a land from the library before revealing top 10 cards slightly improves the quality of Atraxa's reveal, increasing the chance of hitting more card types and better cards.",
      "action_taken": "Passed priority without activating anything, letting Goryo's Vengeance resolve and Atraxa enter with Flooded Strand still on the battlefield.",
      "better_line": "Crack Flooded Strand to thin the deck before Atraxa's ETB reveal of top 10 cards, marginally improving card selection quality.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 117,
      "player": "KimiK2 Vorthos",
      "decision_index": 79,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Declined to pump Psychic Frog by discarding a card before combat damage, missing an extra point of damage against an opponent with no blockers. With the opponent at 16 life and no blockers, discarding a low-value card like Plains to pump Psychic Frog from 1/2 to 2/3 would deal 1 extra damage (9 instead of 8) while still drawing a card from combat damage. Could also have cracked Flooded Strand for deck thinning.",
      "action_taken": "Declined to activate Psychic Frog or crack Flooded Strand during combat.",
      "better_line": "Discard Plains (or the duplicate Psychic Frog) to pump Psychic Frog, dealing an extra point of damage. The combat damage trigger still draws a card either way, so the discard is effectively free value.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 118,
      "player": "KimiK2 Vorthos",
      "decision_index": 79,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "This is Atraxa's ETB trigger resolving, revealing 10 cards where you can select one card of each card type. The first card selected was Force of Negation (instant), which is fine as the instant pick. However, this appears to be just the first selection in a multi-step process. The revealed cards include Atraxa (creature), Shadowy Backstreet/Marsh Flats (land), Ephemerate (instant), March of Otherworldly Light (instant), Winternight Stories (sorcery), and Thoughtseize (sorcery). Force of Negation is a reasonable instant pick, but Ephemerate would arguably be stronger here - it enables re-triggering Atraxa for another 10-card reveal and has rebound for a second use. With Atraxa on the battlefield, Ephemerate generates enormous value and is likely the best instant to select from this reveal.",
      "action_taken": "Selected Force of Negation as the instant from Atraxa's revealed cards.",
      "better_line": "Select Ephemerate as the instant - it can retrigger Atraxa's ETB for massive card advantage and has rebound for a second use, which is far more impactful than a counterspell in this board position where you're ahead.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 125,
      "player": "KimiK2 Vorthos",
      "decision_index": 84,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Discarded the second Psychic Frog to pump the attacking one instead of first playing Flooded Strand as a land drop and then discarding a less valuable card. The second Psychic Frog is a meaningful backup threat, especially with the opponent at 16 life and only one tapped land. They should have played Flooded Strand first (land drop), then discarded Flooded Strand or kept the second Frog for later deployment.",
      "action_taken": "Discarded the second Psychic Frog to activate the on-board Frog's pump ability before making a land drop.",
      "better_line": "Play Flooded Strand as land for the turn first, then if pumping the Frog, discard a less impactful card like Quantum Riddler (hard to cast) or hold the second Frog as a backup threat.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 127,
      "player": "KimiK2 Vorthos",
      "decision_index": 86,
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Discarding the second Psychic Frog to pump the one in play is reasonable for growing the attacker, but discarding Flooded Strand instead would preserve the option of casting a second Psychic Frog later while still getting the +1/+1 counter. The Flooded Strand on the battlefield is already untapped and there's another in play, so the land in hand is relatively low-value. However, KimiK2 already has 5 lands in play plus a Flooded Strand, so the extra land drop may matter less. The redundant Frog could serve as a backup threat if the current one is removed, which is relevant given the opponent has removal in their deck. Discarding a potential backup win condition rather than a marginal land is debatable.",
      "action_taken": "Discarded the second Psychic Frog to pump the one in play",
      "better_line": "Discard Flooded Strand instead to keep the backup Psychic Frog as insurance against removal, while still getting the +1/+1 counter on the attacking Frog",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 127,
      "player": "KimiK2 Vorthos",
      "decision_index": 86,
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Discarding Quantum Riddler to pump Psychic Frog is reasonable for graveyard synergy, but discarding Flooded Strand instead would preserve more optionality. Quantum Riddler has a Warp cost of {1}{U} which could be cast more easily than the full 3UU, and with the opponent at 12 life and KimiK2 holding two Force of Negations for protection, keeping the Riddler as a potential threat while discarding the less impactful Flooded Strand (KimiK2 already has 6 lands including an untapped Flooded Strand on board) would be slightly better. However, the reanimation angle with Goryo's Vengeance in the graveyard doesn't work since Quantum Riddler isn't legendary, and the Warp ability does let you cast it from graveyard, so there's some merit to putting it there. This is close but slightly suboptimal.",
      "action_taken": "Discarded Quantum Riddler to pump Psychic Frog",
      "better_line": "Discard Flooded Strand instead — already have 6 lands including a Flooded Strand on board, and Quantum Riddler's Warp {1}{U} from graveyard is available regardless but keeping it in hand preserves the option to hard-cast it. Flooded Strand provides minimal additional value at this stage of the game.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 132,
      "player": "KimiK2 Vorthos",
      "decision_index": 89,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Discarded Solitude, which is one of the most important cards in hand. With only 2 lands on turn 14, Solitude's evoke ability (exile a white card from hand) provides free removal and pairs with Ephemerate for a devastating combo: evoke Solitude to exile opponent's creature, then Ephemerate it to keep it on battlefield permanently and get a second exile trigger. This is the classic Modern Solitude + Ephemerate line.",
      "action_taken": "Discarded Solitude to hand size",
      "better_line": "Discard Overlord of the Balemurk, which requires 5 mana to hardcast (unreachable with 2 lands) or impending for 1B which takes 5 turns to become a creature. Keeping Solitude preserves the Solitude + Ephemerate combo as a powerful free interaction.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 147,
      "player": "GPT5n Spike",
      "decision_index": 95,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Playing Hallowed Fountain before casting Thoughtseize is suboptimal sequencing. The player should cast Thoughtseize first (they already have black mana from Godless Shrine) to see the opponent's hand before committing to a land drop. Additionally, playing Flooded Strand instead of Hallowed Fountain would be better since the fetch land can be cracked later to enable Revolt for Fatal Push or to thin the deck, and avoids the 2-life payment decision. More importantly, with a 6/7 menace Psychic Frog and the opponent at 12 life with only 2 lands and no creatures, the priority should be casting Thoughtseize first to strip any potential answer (like a board wipe or removal spell) before combat, rather than playing a land that doesn't change available mana for the Thoughtseize.",
      "action_taken": "Played Hallowed Fountain as the first action of the turn.",
      "better_line": "Cast Thoughtseize first using existing mana (Godless Shrine provides B) to see opponent's hand and strip their best answer before combat, then play the land drop afterward.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 147,
      "player": "KimiK2 Vorthos",
      "decision_index": 98,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The player is being Thoughtseized (opponent's spell on the stack forcing them to discard a nonland card). They chose to discard Force of Negation, but their reasoning says they're discarding it to Psychic Frog's ability to grow it. However, this is a Thoughtseize - the opponent is choosing what to discard. Wait - the message says 'Select a card (discard cost)' which suggests this is actually the player choosing what to discard. Looking more carefully: Thoughtseize is on the stack, but the player is KimiK2 Vorthos selecting a card. Thoughtseize says 'Target player reveals their hand. You choose a nonland card from it.' So GPT5n Spike cast Thoughtseize targeting KimiK2 Vorthos, but the system is asking KimiK2 Vorthos to select - this seems like the target is choosing, which shouldn't happen with Thoughtseize. Actually, looking at the 'After' text: 'KimiK2 Vorthos discards Force of Negation (source: Psychic Frog)' - so this is actually a Psychic Frog activation happening while Thoughtseize is on the stack. The player is discarding to Psychic Frog to pump it in response to Thoughtseize. This is actually reasonable - pumping the Frog before Thoughtseize resolves. But by discarding Force of Negation to the Frog, they're giving up a powerful free counterspell. The Flooded Strand would be the better discard to Psychic Frog since it's a land and provides less value from hand. However, Psychic Frog's ability says 'Discard a card' - it doesn't restrict to nonland. Discarding Flooded Strand would also give a +1/+1 counter while preserving Force of Negation. Thoughtseize will then resolve and the opponent will choose from the remaining cards - but keeping Force of Negation would be much more valuable than keeping Flooded Strand.",
      "action_taken": "Discarded Force of Negation to Psychic Frog's pump ability in response to Thoughtseize on the stack.",
      "better_line": "Discard Flooded Strand to Psychic Frog instead, preserving Force of Negation which is far more valuable as a free counterspell. Thoughtseize would then likely take the remaining Force of Negation, but the player would still have gotten the same pump while keeping a land drop option if Thoughtseize had been countered or if the opponent took something else.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 149,
      "player": "KimiK2 Vorthos",
      "decision_index": 101,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "KimiK2 Vorthos resolved a Thoughtseize against GPT5n Spike and chose to discard Thoughtseize — a 1-mana sorcery that is far less threatening than the other options in the opponent's hand. The opponent has Teferi, Time Raveler (which shuts down instant-speed interaction and draws cards), Overlord of the Balemurk (a powerful 5/5 that mills and recurs creatures), Ephemerate (which can rebuy ETB effects repeatedly), and two Fatal Pushes that can kill Psychic Frog. Teferi is particularly devastating because it prevents KimiK2 from casting Force of Negation (their only card in hand besides a land) at instant speed, and its -3 can bounce Psychic Frog while drawing a card. Taking Thoughtseize over Teferi or Overlord of the Balemurk is a massive misplay.",
      "action_taken": "Chose to discard Thoughtseize from opponent's hand",
      "better_line": "Should have taken Teferi, Time Raveler, which shuts down Force of Negation's alternate cost timing, bounces Psychic Frog, and generates card advantage. Alternatively, Overlord of the Balemurk would also have been a strong pick as a major threat.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 150,
      "player": "KimiK2 Vorthos",
      "decision_index": 103,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Psychic Frog was already at sufficient power (6+) to deal lethal damage to the opponent at 6 life with no blockers. Discarding a card to pump it further wastes a card for pure overkill damage.",
      "action_taken": "Activated Psychic Frog's discard ability to pump it beyond lethal, unnecessarily spending a card.",
      "better_line": "Simply pass without activating the ability - the Frog already deals lethal combat damage. Preserve the card in hand in case something unexpected happens or for future value.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 162,
      "player": "KimiK2 Vorthos",
      "decision_index": 110,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Discarded Force of Negation to pump Psychic Frog when Flooded Strand (a land) was available as a discard. With the opponent at 6 life and potentially able to topdeck answers, Force of Negation provides crucial protection against noncreature spells that could swing the game. Both cards give the same +1/+1 counter when discarded.",
      "action_taken": "Discarded Force of Negation to Psychic Frog's pump ability",
      "better_line": "Discard Flooded Strand instead, preserving Force of Negation as protection against any noncreature spell the opponent might cast to stabilize",
      "type": "blunder",
